# Keep the test suite out of the published sdist. Tests live in the top-level sdk_tests/
# directory, OUTSIDE the agentx_sdk package -- that LOCATION is the real guard: find_packages()
# keeps them out of the wheel, and setuptools' sdist builder does not pull a top-level
# non-package dir into the sdist either. So on the current sdk_tests/ name the prune lines
# below are a no-op; they are kept as defensive belt-and-suspenders, so that a future rename
# to a name setuptools auto-includes (test/ or tests/) cannot silently re-add tests to the
# sdist. The public PyPI + MIT-mirror surface stays shipping-code-only.
prune sdk_tests
recursive-exclude sdk_tests *

# THE EXAMPLES, ON PURPOSE, AND ONLY THE SOURCE FILES.
#
# The sdist is what the public MIT mirror is generated from (its SOURCES.txt surface), so
# until now the mirror carried the SDK with no example of using it, and every CLI screen that
# wanted to point a reader at one had nothing reachable to point at: a `pip install` user has
# no repo, and the monorepo is private. `agentx demo --audit` covers the reader who wants to
# RUN one; this covers the reader who wants to READ one.
#
# Scoped to *.py so the ledger and caches an example leaves behind when somebody runs it
# (.agentx.db, __pycache__/, .agentx/) can never be swept into a release. The mirror is a
# published surface and a stray ledger on it is a leak, not a mess.
#
# 🔴 THE INCLUDE LIST IS REVIEWED AS PUBLISHED COPY, NOT AS A FILE LIST. The first version of
# it was a glob, and the glob was wrong three ways at once: `ts-guard/` documents the gateway
# wire contract and cites `backend/gateway.py:5703` in its comments, which republishes the
# layout and line offsets of the CLOSED half of this product; it carried a Vitest suite onto
# a surface this file's own first paragraph says is shipping-code-only; and every example
# imports `python-dotenv`, which `install_requires` does not bring, so the scripts were
# runnable-looking and not runnable. `sdk_tests/test_published_example_surface.py` now
# asserts all three against the real files, and it found a fourth (an example printing a path
# inside our private `eval/`) that review did not.
#
# ts-guard is deliberately NOT here. It is a demonstration of the gateway's contract, and the
# gateway is the part nobody outside can read.
include examples/*.py
include examples/requirements.txt
include examples/README.md
include examples/mcp/*.py
include examples/mcp/*.json
include examples/mcp/README.md

global-exclude *.pyc
global-exclude .agentx.db
